# Golden results for callbacks.dat. `dat-conformance examples --bless` rewrites this file.
# `run` and `test` sections must match on the interpreter and compiled.

=== check
status: 1
--- stderr
error[E0651]: `mut` parameter `xs` can't have a default value
 --> callbacks.dat:4:15
4 | function fill(mut xs: list<int> = []): void {
  |               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ a call that leaves it out has no value of its own for the function to change

error[E0305]: `x > 1` has no effect
  --> callbacks.dat:10:28
10 |   print(xs.filter((x) => { x > 1; }));
   |                            ^^^^^ `<arrow>` returns a bool, and this is its last statement
fix (review): return it
10 |   print(xs.filter((x) => { return x > 1; }));

error[E0252]: this callback returns nothing, and its result is needed
  --> callbacks.dat:11:16
11 |   print(xs.map((x) => { return; }));
   |                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ return a value from it; to only do something for each element, use `forEach` or a loop

error[E0001]: an arrow function's parameters go in parentheses
  --> callbacks.dat:12:16
12 |   print(xs.map(x => x * 2));
   |                ^
fix: write `(x)`
12 |   print(xs.map((x) => x * 2));

error[E0851]: list<int> has no method `sorted`
  --> callbacks.dat:13:12
13 |   print(xs.sorted(), xs.reversed(), xs.size);
   |            ^^^^^^
fix (review): use `toSorted`
13 |   print(xs.toSorted(), xs.reversed(), xs.size);

error[E0851]: list<int> has no method `reversed`
  --> callbacks.dat:13:25
13 |   print(xs.sorted(), xs.reversed(), xs.size);
   |                         ^^^^^^^^
fix (review): use `toReversed`
13 |   print(xs.sorted(), xs.toReversed(), xs.size);

error[E0851]: list<int> has no field `size`
  --> callbacks.dat:13:40
13 |   print(xs.sorted(), xs.reversed(), xs.size);
   |                                        ^^^^ list<int> has `length`
fix: read `length`
13 |   print(xs.sorted(), xs.reversed(), xs.length);

error[E0851]: map<string, int> has no field `length`
  --> callbacks.dat:15:11
15 |   print(m.length);
   |           ^^^^^^ map<string, int> has `size`
fix: read `size`
15 |   print(m.size);

error[E0350]: `ys.length` can't be changed
  --> callbacks.dat:17:3
17 |   ys.length = 0;
   |   ^^^^^^^^^ the `length` follows from the elements; remove or add elements to change it

error[E0703]: `ys` is in use by the loop
  --> callbacks.dat:19:10
19 |     y += ys.length;
   |          ^^ inside a `for (mut ...)` loop, the list can only be reached through the loop variable
10 errors, 3 fixable with dat fix

